title: LSP Server

!! Serving an editor from the running wiki

`--lsp` starts a Language Server Protocol server inside a TiddlyWiki process. It is read only: it answers questions about the wiki and never writes to it.

!!! Starting

Usually your editor starts it: the VS Code extension runs the wiki's own LSP server as a child of the window, see [[LSP Configuration]]. By hand:

```
tiddlywiki ./mywiki --lsp
```

The server listens on `127.0.0.1:6009`, or on a free port when 6009 is taken, and records the port in `.tw-mcp/lsp` in the wiki folder, where an editor finds it.

|! Parameter |! Default |! Meaning |
|`port=<n>` |6009 |TCP port to listen on. A port given here is not replaced when taken; `port=0` asks for any free one |
|`host=<h>` |127.0.0.1 |Interface to bind |
|`stdio` |off |Serve over stdin and stdout instead of a socket. Cannot be combined with `--mcp` or `pipe=`, see [[LSP and MCP]] |
|`pipe=<name>` |off |Connect to a named pipe the editor created and serve that one session. The process never writes to disk, reads in tiddler files changed on disk, and exits when the pipe closes |
|`label=<name>` |`lsp-<wiki folder>` |Name of the server in its own log and in the MCP server's. Without it, `label` from the `lsp` section of `tiddlywiki.info` |

!!! Which wiki answers

The first log line names the wiki the server loaded, and the same line reaches the editor's log once it connects:

```
[tw-lsp @lsp-mywiki] Wiki /home/me/mywiki; tiddlers folder /home/me/mywiki/tiddlers; 812 tiddlers, 1406 shadows
```

!!! Completion

Typing `[[` or `{{`, or the value of a widget attribute that takes a title such as `<$link to="`, offers tiddler titles, narrowed as you keep typing. A title containing spaces completes correctly, because the server states the range to replace rather than leaving the editor to guess where the word began.

System tiddlers stay out of the list until the prefix starts with `$:/`, which also reaches shadow tiddlers.

Matching is by prefix and ignores case, from the first character typed. See [[LSP Capabilities]] for the exact rules.

!!! Diagnostics

A link, a `<$link to="...">` or a transclusion whose target does not exist is marked as a warning naming the missing title. Shadow tiddlers count as targets.

A call whose name nothing in the wiki defines, such as a misspelt macro or widget, is marked as a hint with a quick fix to the closest existing names, and so is a filter step that names no operator. See [[LSP Diagnostics]].

Left alone: external addresses, filters (`{{{ ... }}}` included), macro arguments and other attribute values, inline code, fenced code blocks, and the field header of a `.tid` file, where a `tags` field routinely names tiddlers that do not exist yet.

!!! Hover

Resting the cursor on a filter expression runs it against the live wiki and reports what it matches right now. A filter you have not finished typing says so rather than claiming to match nothing.

!!! Unsaved text

The server mirrors the buffers your editor has open, so every answer covers text you have not saved. The older side of a diff is answered too, but it gets no warnings, quick fixes or rename, and never counts as the wiki's own text.

